"""Section IV. Placeholders written as «key» are filled from measured statistics."""

PROTO = [
("h1", "IV. DATA AND EXPERIMENTAL PROTOCOL"),
("h2", "A. Datasets"),
("p", "*TRACE-10.* Public benchmarks do not report where the discriminative evidence of a clip lies, so a claim that a sampler finds short events cannot be checked on them directly. TRACE-10 (transient-event recognition in long clips) was generated for this purpose and is released with the code. Each video has $R=192$ frames of $112\\times112$ pixels showing two or three polygons of random shape and grey level drifting slowly over a smooth random texture, under a slow camera random walk. One object, the actor, is drawn with a thin dark outline and otherwise follows the same drift as the distractors. At a random time it performs a single out-and-back micro-event that defines the class: a jab of 34 pixels to the right, left, up or down, a clockwise or counter-clockwise tilt of $90^{\\circ}$, a growth pulse or a shrink pulse, each lasting 10 to 16 frames (5–8% of the video), plus two slow classes in which the right or left jab takes 64 to 96 frames. Because every event returns the object to its starting state, frames taken before and after the event are identical in distribution, and the label can only be read from frames inside it. Half of the videos also contain a six-frame camera bump that moves the whole image by up to ten pixels and carries no label information. The fast and slow jabs have the same displacement, so they can be told apart only from elapsed time. The split is 2000/400/600 videos (train/validation/test) with balanced classes and disjoint random seeds."),
("p", "*HMDB51.* The standard split 1 [@hmdb] provides «hm_trval» training and «hm_te» test clips over 51 classes. Clips cut from the same source video share a group prefix in their file names; a group-aware partition moves whole groups, about 15% of the training clips per class, into a validation set of «hm_va» clips, leaving «hm_tr» for training. This prevents near-duplicate clips from leaking between training and model selection, and the official test split is already group-disjoint."),
("p", "*Something-Something V2 subset.* The public evaluation subset of SSv2 [@ssv2] distributed with the MTEB video benchmark contains «ss_n» clips over 174 motion-defined classes (15 to 32 per class). Because only this subset is openly redistributable, a stratified 70/10/20 split with a fixed seed yields «ss_tr», «ss_va» and «ss_te» clips. With roughly 22 training clips per class, absolute accuracy on this subset is expected to be far below published full-scale numbers; it is used for relative comparisons on a motion-centred label set, and Table {tab:data} reports it as such."),
("h2", "B. Preprocessing and Leakage Control"),
("p", "Every clip is decoded once. The probe of Section III-B runs on all raw frames. A lattice of $T$ candidate frames ($T=48$ for TRACE-10, $T=32$ otherwise) is drawn uniformly over the clip; each candidate is resized on its short side to 112 pixels, centre-cropped and normalized with ImageNet statistics, and encoded under bfloat16 autocast by a frozen DINOv2 ViT-S/14 [@dinov2], which yields one class token and $8\\times8$ patch tokens of width 384. Token width is reduced to $D=192$ by a PCA fitted on training clips only (explained variance «pca_evr»). For the temporal model the $8\\times8$ grid is average-pooled to $N=16$ regions of $28\\times28$ pixels, and probe energies are summed over the same regions. Caching the lattice tokens once makes all compared methods read identical features; a method that selects $K$ frames is charged for $K$ encoder calls in every cost figure. No augmentation other than the stratified offsets of {eq:quant} (or random positions within segments for uniform sampling) is used, so that differences between methods cannot come from different augmentation pipelines."),
("h2", "C. Compared Methods"),
("p", "All methods share the frozen encoder, the cached tokens, the optimizer and the schedule. Except for the TSN-style consensus, all use the same two-layer temporal transformer, so differences come from what is fed into it. The baselines cover five families: (i) classical consensus, TSN-style mean of frame class tokens [@tsn]; (ii) frame-level temporal transformers on uniformly sampled frames (Uniform-CLS) and on all $T$ lattice frames (Dense-CLS, four to six times the encoder cost); (iii) joint space-time attention over every patch of uniformly sampled frames (Uniform-Full) [@timesformer, vivit] and with motion-guided sampling (MG-Full) [@mgsampler]; (iv) token reducers at the same budget on uniform frames, namely random dropping, learned per-frame selection in the manner of STTS [@stts], bipartite merging (ToMe) [@tome], class-attentive keeping with fusion (EViT) [@evit] and repeated-patch removal in the manner of RLT [@rlt]; and (v) the naive combination of motion-guided sampling with learned token selection (MG-STTS), the closest competitor that a reviewer would assemble from existing parts. Budgeted methods receive exactly $1+K+32$ input tokens at $K=8$ and $\\beta=0.25$, that is, 41 tokens against 137 for full attention; KAIROS uses $B=24$ patch tokens plus eight context tokens to reach the same total. STTS is trained with the straight-through gate of {eq:gate} instead of the perturbed top-$K$ operator of the original paper, and RLT is approximated at fixed budget by keeping the first frame in full and the most-changed patches elsewhere; these choices make all reducers comparable at equal token count."),
("h2", "D. Implementation Details"),
("p", "Table {tab:impl} lists the settings. Training uses AdamW (learning rate $10^{-3}$, weight decay 0.05), a one-epoch linear warm-up followed by cosine decay, batch size 64, 20 epochs and label smoothing 0.1. The checkpoint with the best validation accuracy is evaluated once on the test split. Every configuration is trained with seeds 0, 1 and 2 for the main comparison and the factorial ablation, with seeds 0 and 1 for component removals (seed 0 only on SSv2), and with seed 0 for sensitivity sweeps. Experiments ran on a two-core CPU with PyTorch 2.15 and timm 1.0, using one thread per run; the notebook reproduces every step on a free Colab instance."),
("h2", "E. Evaluation Metrics"),
("p", "Top-1 accuracy is the primary metric; top-5 accuracy is added for SSv2 and macro-F1 for all datasets. Calibration is measured with the expected calibration error over $Q=15$ equal-width confidence bins [@guo],"),
("eq", "\\mathrm{ECE}=\\sum_{q=1}^{Q}\\frac{\\left|\\mathcal{G}_{q}\\right|}{n}\\left|\\operatorname{acc}\\left(\\mathcal{G}_{q}\\right)-\\operatorname{conf}\\left(\\mathcal{G}_{q}\\right)\\right|", "ece"),
("p", "where $\\mathcal{G}_q$ is the set of test videos whose top probability falls in bin $q$ and $n$ is the number of test videos; negative log-likelihood is reported alongside. On TRACE-10 the ground-truth event interval $[t_0,t_1)$ is known, and the burst hit rate measures how much of the frame budget lands on it,"),
("eq", "H=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\mathbb{1}\\left[t_{0}\\leq c_{j_{k}}<t_{1}+\\delta\\right]", "hit"),
("p", "with $\\delta$ equal to one lattice step, because the energy accumulated at a lattice point describes the interval that precedes it. The fraction of videos with at least two samples inside the event is reported as well, since two samples are the minimum needed to see a direction of change. Cost is reported as measured GFLOPs per video (encoder, probe and temporal model) and as single-thread CPU latency."),
("h2", "F. Statistical Analysis"),
("p", "Results are reported as mean $\\pm$ standard deviation over seeds. For each dataset, the difference between KAIROS and a baseline is tested on the test videos with a paired bootstrap (10 000 resamples) of the seed-averaged per-video correctness, giving a 95% confidence interval and a one-sided $p$-value, and with McNemar's test [@mcnemar] on the majority vote over seeds. The Holm procedure [@holm] controls the family-wise error over the baselines of each dataset. Across the nine dataset-seed blocks, a Friedman test [@friedman] checks whether the methods differ in rank, and the Nemenyi critical difference [@demsar]"),
("eq", "\\mathrm{CD}=q_{\\alpha}\\sqrt{\\frac{M(M+1)}{6N_{b}}}", "cd"),
("p", "with $M$ methods, $N_b$ blocks and the Studentized-range quantile $q_{\\alpha}$ at $\\alpha=0.05$, identifies which rank differences are significant. A Wilcoxon signed-rank test [@wilcoxon] over the nine blocks gives a complementary paired comparison for each baseline. Effect sizes are reported as absolute accuracy differences in percentage points."),
]
